  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe will use a coordinate descent-based optimization approach starting from the provided initial solution, followed by a gradient-based refinement phase to improve the maximum overlap. This approach aims to systematically reduce the maximum overlap value C5 by fractionally adjusting function values in regions that contribute most to the maximum overlap. The coordinate descent phase ensures local optimality, while the gradient phase improves convergence and escaping local minima.\n\nThe key improvements from the previous approach are:\n- Cleaner separation of steps\n- More systematic coordinate adjustment\n- Improved repair mechanism with better integral balancing\n- More efficient computation through careful timing management\n- Better handling of the objective function through direct gradient computation\n\nThe method avoids local minima by perturbing solutions when necessary and uses a three-stage optimization process.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Representation: 1D function on [0,2] represented as n-point samples with dx=2/n\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = max_lag \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx computed via discrete correlation\n- Constraints: \n  - h[i] \u2208 [0,1] for all i\n  - \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 exactly (integral constraint)\n\nIntegral normalization: \u2211h[i] * (2/n_points) = 1, so \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n\nRepair approach:\n  Target integral = n_points/2\n  Adjust by (target - actual)/n ratio\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nAll stages use n_points=560 unless specified otherwise.\n\nStages:\n1. Repair(initial) = clip and balance to meet integral constraint\n2. local_optimization(x, budget_s):\n   a. Record execution start\n   b. Set phases:\n        phase1 = 0.6*total_time (coordinate descent)\n        phase2 = 0.4*total_time (gradient+refinement)\n   c. Set timer\n   d. Perform coordinate descent:\n        For each value index i (0 to n-1):\n            - Fix other coordinates \n            - Solve for optimal h[i] at each phase segment\n            Each segment: T_d = budget_s/phase_seg\n    e. Implement repair in between segments to maintain feasibility\n   f. Verify results\n4. Validation/return:\n   - Verify \u2211h == n_points/2\n   - Perform full correlation with dx normalization\n   - Return improved solution if better\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Main approach uses coordinate descent first\n   - Alternative phase orders available:\n        Option A: Gradient descent first\n        Option B: Multi-resolution search similar to parent\n   - Selection varies repair parameters based on:\n        - Fraction remaining in budget\n        - Historical repair success rate\n\n5. Computational budget and robustness:\n   - Monitor time remaining in each subphase\n   - Time budget checks after every step\n   - Time-based termination after 1100s\n   - Provide fallback if repair exceeds performance expectations\n   - Repair fallback uses sliding window balancing\n   - External integration through global variables\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Check \u2211h == n_points/2 within 1e-6 tolerance\n   - Verify all h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n   - Compute C5 via: np.max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full'))*(2/len(h))\n   - If invalid candidate, repair and recompute\n   - Return (h, c5, n_points)\n   - Candidates must satisfy both integral and bound constraints",
